`isdefined` returns `True` (it's called a `condition test`). So `\Scopus` is defined when `\if xxx` is `True` (for what Python cares it could even be `\anything`. If nothing is defined Python will think it is false and continue to the next line. And this is the very first line in `pywac_scitation\scitation_.py`.

The \em{condition} here is whether you have installed the necessary package, but not if you actually loaded it, and this is what you want when calling `pkg_resources.get_distribution` to do.

Once you load `Scopus` (or whatever it's called in python) it can load all the packages it contains (and the packages in packages as well ...) and load `docstore.papers` which it finds in `docstore`.papers (you will notice a lot of python import is quite long ... Python is verbose...). So the whole import procedure could potentially result in a "circular load". You can prevent this with

\begin{code}
try:
    import _scitation.docstore.papers
except ImportError:
    pass
\end{code}

